+### Result structuring (AD-9 — the deterministic spine)
+
+`structure_reference_call_result` maps the flat `draft_answers` (key → JSON value) onto the `ReferenceCallResult v1` shape. **Deterministic, no LLM** (AD-9). Per-slot `verification` (§5.3 + the §15 per-slot-variant decision):
+
+- **`landlord_ref`**: `identity_confirmed`, `relationship_to_applicant`, `tenancy_confirmed`, `tenancy_period{from,to,as_stated}`, `rent_amount_confirmed`, `rent_payment_record`, `property_condition`, `notice_given_properly`, `deposit_returned`, `would_rent_again`, `free_text_signals[]`. Unanswered → `null`/`"unknown"`.
+- **`employer_ref`**: `identity_confirmed`, `relationship_to_applicant`(Manager/HR/Colleague/Other), `employment_period{…,as_stated}`, `role_confirmed`, `salary_confirmed`, `would_rehire`, `free_text_signals[]`. Tenancy fields null.
+- **`character_ref`**: `identity_confirmed`, `relationship{+capacity}`, `known_duration`, `character_statement`, `would_stand_again`, `free_text_signals[]`.
+
+`ai_summary` (deterministic rendering of the verification block):
+- `headline`: one-line templated from the headline answers (tenancy confirmed + payment record for landlord; employment confirmed + would-rehire for employer; the character statement gist for character).
+- `summary`: a short deterministic prose rendering of the key fields.
+- `confidence`: `high` when every required field answered; `medium` when ≥1 "unknown"/unanswered; `low` when the set is sparse. (rc3-7's fraud signals are a separate dimension, not confidence.)
+
+`compliance`: `{gdpr_notice_given: true, referee_channel_preference: null, recording_consent: "not_recorded"}` (v1; voice fields v2-reserved, absent).
+
+`fraud_signals`: structured with the `form_session` block (`completion_seconds` from `form_opened_at`→`submitted_at`, the `_focus_seconds` the form carried); line-type/DB-reuse signals are rc3-7 — leave their slots null/absent (AD-9 absence contract).
+
+`attempts[]`: the row's existing attempt log (read at submit, rendered into the result). `outcome: "form_completed"`. `channel: "form"`. `schema_version: "refcall-v1"`.
+
+### Objection stickiness + single writer (the load-bearing edge — AC4/AC5)
+
+- The stop POST calls **one** `apply_objection` helper that, in a single guarded transaction-shaped flow, runs the guarded `UPDATE … WHERE status='contact_initiated'` (or the sticky-aware variant) and — **only if that UPDATE returned a row** — inserts the `reference_objection_log` row. A re-tap on an already-`objected` row: the guarded UPDATE is a 0-row no-op → the handler renders the branded terminal page and does **not** insert a second objection_log row. (The objection_log is "written at objection time" — once.)
+- Stickiness is the SQL guard's job (AD-14): the `WHERE status='contact_initiated'` means a late bounce/sweep/second-submit on an `objected` row matches 0 rows and stands down. The integration test (AC5) flips a row to `objected`, then fires a second stop POST + simulates a late submit, asserting the row + outcome + objection_log are unchanged.
+- `reference_objection_log` is **not** FK-coupled (plain UUID) so it survives both parent deletions (§9.2). `payload_ref` points at the captured web-request evidence (tap IP/UA + token binding) — reuse the evidentiary IP/UA capture pattern (§7.1 reused list).
+
+### Abuse posture per route (AC7 — AD-15)
+
+- **submit** (`POST /reference/:token`): the full answer-timing posture is too loose for a multi-field submit; use the resend-style 2s floor (single-action submission) with the TTL-aware stale cap. Honeypot + timing → silent-drop with the thank-you page (fake success; nothing persisted).
+- **decline / stop / wrong-person POSTs**: 2s floor (single-action pages), same stale cap. Honeypot filled → fake-success page **without** the state change (the bot sees the confirmation a clean referee would see; the row is untouched — critical for stop, where a fake object would silently opt out real referees if the token leaked).
+- The honeypot/timing helpers (`is_honeypot_filled`, `is_timing_suspicious_within`) already exist — reuse, do not fork.
+
+### Route registration — the prefix match IS the design (AC8)
+
+The three security registries match `["reference", ..]` (prefix) by **intentional design** (the docstrings: "Prefix match so any future token-in-a-deeper-segment route is still covered"). Adding per-arm entries would either be dead code (after the prefix arm, in Gleam's ordered case) or shadow the prefix. So: **no per-arm registry edits**. The work is (a) the router arms (the one per-arm registry) and (b) **tests that prove the prefix match bites** for each new route — exercise each POST through the full `router.handle_request` + middleware stack asserting 2xx/3xx (not a 403 CSRF rejection) and a redacted path in the access-log/Sentry shapes. This is the truthful satisfaction of the rc3-3 lens-guard's concern (it flags *missing coverage*, and the prefix match + tests provide it).
+
+### Notification dispatch — landlord_id/vacancy_id lookup
+
+`get_form_context_by_form_token.sql` already joins `vacancy` + `landlord`; extend the SELECT with `v.user_id::text AS landlord_id` and `a.vacancy_id::text AS vacancy_id`. The submit/decline/stop handlers then have everything `notify_reference_*` needs (landlord_id, vacancy_id, application_id, referee_name=`contact_name`, applicant_full_name, origin_domain). No second lookup query.
+
+### `refused` is v1 here despite §4.1's `[v2-reserved]` comment (doc-drift)
+
+The architecture §4.1 status enum comments `refused` as `[v2-reserved, terminal]`, but: (a) the rc3-4 AC explicitly declines → `refused`; (b) the `reference_call_status` enum value exists; (c) the `reference_call_outcome` enum value exists; (d) the rc2-1 notification migration comment reads `"reference_declined — form decline route (A8: distinct from objected)"`. The `[v2-reserved]` comment predates the A8 decision that brought decline into v1. Implement decline → `refused`; flag the doc-drift in the PR.
+
+### Constraints (RT-specific — APPLY here)
+- **No em-dashes in user-facing copy** (convention, review-enforced; r2 verified zero held). Rephrase to periods.
+- **SSR never requires JS** — decline/stop/wrong-person are plain POSTs (GET confirm + POST act), reachable without JS.
+- **Objection stickiness is load-bearing** (AD-6/AD-14) — the hardest edge case; lean on `bmad-review-edge-case-hunter`.
+- **`reference_objection_log` single writer** — only `apply_objection` writes it, at objection time, once.
+- **No `let assert` in production; explicit HTTP timeouts on any outbound call; Squirrel for all SQL.**
+- **Test-DB port 54321 contention** — run your own container (`docker run -d --name rt-rc3-4-test-db -p 5432X:5432 postgres:16-alpine`) and pass `TEST_DB_PORT`.
+- **Prove every new guard/test BITES** — negative control (flip one assertion, confirm the expected failure, revert).
+
+### Pattern fidelity — mirror these exactly
+- The `handle_resend` / `resend_with_send` shape (injectable send seam, guarded UPDATE, audit, state-page routing) is the template for the exit-route handlers.
+- `classify_row` + `TokenState` already cover every status — reuse, do not fork.
+- The `dispatch()` + `maybe_send_email()` notification pattern (see `notify_application_scored`).
+- The `view_state_card` page shape for confirmations; the `view_expired`/`view_resend_failed` honeypot+timing POST form for the GET-confirm-then-POST-act pages.
